PART 1️⃣ — What is Kafka (REAL-TIME EXPLANATION)

Think of Kafka like this (Real World)

> Kafka is a high-throughput, fault-tolerant event log used to move facts between systems in real time.

Analogy (Very Important)
📒 Kafka = Distributed Commit Log

- Append-only
- Ordered
- Immutable
- Replayable

What Kafka is used for in real systems

| Use Case            | Example                  |
|---------------------|--------------------------|
| Event streaming     | OrderPlaced, PaymentDone |
| Async communication | Microservices            |
| Data pipelines      | DB → Analytics           |
| Event sourcing      | System state from events |

Kafka in production (E-commerce)

User places order
   ↓
OrderService → Kafka → Payment, Inventory, Analytics

Kafka does NOT process business logic  
Kafka stores & delivers events

PART 2️⃣ — Kafka Core Components (Practical)

1️⃣ Producer
- Sends events

2️⃣ Topic
- Category of events
- Example: order-events

3️⃣ Partition
- Ordered log
- Parallelism unit

4️⃣ Consumer
- Reads events

5️⃣ Consumer Group
- Scales consumers horizontally

Example Topic

order-events
 ├─ partition-0
 ├─ partition-1
 └─ partition-2

Ordering Guarantee
✔ Ordered within a partition  
❌ Not ordered across partitions

PART 3️⃣ — What is Controller in Kafka? (Important & Often Confusing)

Kafka Controller (NOT Spring Controller)

> Kafka Controller is a broker responsible for cluster metadata management.

What Controller does
✔ Leader election for partitions  
✔ Detects broker failure  
✔ Assigns partitions  
✔ Manages ISR (in-sync replicas)

Example (Broker Failure)


Broker-1 (leader) ❌ dies
        ↓
Controller detects
        ↓
Elects new leader (Broker-2)

⚠️ Only ONE controller per cluster

ZooKeeper vs KRaft

| Kafka Version | Controller |
|-----|----|
| Old | ZooKeeper elects |
| New | KRaft (Raft protocol) |

Important Clarification
❌ Kafka Controller ≠ REST Controller  
❌ Kafka Controller ≠ Microservice Controller  


PART 4️⃣ — Saga State Machine Implementation (REAL JAVA)

Why Saga State Machine?

> To track saga progress, failures, retries, and compensations explicitly

Saga States (Example)

java
public enum SagaState {
    STARTED,
    PAYMENT_COMPLETED,
    INVENTORY_RESERVED,
    SHIPPING_CREATED,
    COMPLETED,
    COMPENSATING,
    FAILED
}

Saga Entity

java
@Entity
public class OrderSaga {

    @Id
    private String orderId;

    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)
    private SagaState state;
}

Saga Event Handler

java
@Service
public class SagaEventHandler {

    @Transactional
    public void handlePaymentCompleted(String orderId) {
        OrderSaga saga = repo.findById(orderId).get();

        if (saga.getState() == SagaState.STARTED) {
            saga.setState(SagaState.PAYMENT_COMPLETED);
            repo.save(saga);
            publishInventoryCommand(orderId);
        }
    }
}

Compensation Logic

java
@Transactional
public void handleInventoryFailed(String orderId) {
    OrderSaga saga = repo.findById(orderId).get();
    saga.setState(SagaState.COMPENSATING);
    repo.save(saga);
    publishRefundCommand(orderId);
}

✔ Explicit  
✔ Restart-safe  
✔ Observable  

